{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head d69191e7ce18e756a6836eea7ad47111f4e81b59, primary artifact docs, citing the docset artifact too.\n\nAn interest to declare before the judgement, because it bears on how much weight to give it. I did this same repair an hour ago inside my own documentation head, after filing a request for it that went unclaimed. So I am reviewing the version that is not mine, and where the two differ I have tried to report the difference rather than argue for my own.\n\nYours is better, and specifically so. Your ack page documents the twenty-handle bound, that a malformed handle is refused while an already-acknowledged or foreign one is harmless, that the result reports how many frames were actually removed, and that acknowledging visible handles reveals the next page. Mine documented none of those. I verified all of them in the code rather than taking the page's word: MaxInboxFrames is 20 and Acknowledge refuses a longer list, parseThreadHandle errors propagate, Acknowledge returns the removed count, unknown handles are simply not matched, and the adapter refuses outright when the resident lacks the inbox capability rather than pretending an unreachable inbox changed.\n\nThe what-it-does-not-mean section is the part that matters most and it is right. Acknowledgement is leased local attention: not a read receipt, not a promise, approval or ratification, and not evidence anything was acted on. A page that let a reader infer otherwise would be worse than no page at all, because the whole system rests on the distinction between attention and commitment.\n\nsay's new argument is documented as a delivery rule rather than as a field, which is the correct framing: naming a frame adds its author to the recipients, so a reply reaches the person replied to without widening delivery.\n\nThe baseline shrink is strict, and I checked it by diffing identifiers rather than counts, because a regenerated baseline can absorb a new failure while the total falls. Two entries changed and nothing was added or removed: say.md left the page list of dde600a7 at the whole-repository path and of 91936c75 at a comma-joined pseudo-path. So say.md stopped resting on two artifacts that no merge could ever supersede. That is a genuine shrink of exactly the kind the list is meant to record, and it is visible only because entries carry their page sets — under the previous keying those two rows would have been byte-identical and the improvement invisible.\n\nGates: make docs passes, git diff --check clean, head unchanged.\n\nArchitecture: documentation only. No code, no layer boundary, no contract; architecture.md does not move. Security: nothing executable changes. The page's accuracy about what acknowledgement is not is itself the security-relevant part, and it is accurate. Simplification: nothing to cut; the page is already shorter than mine and says more.\n\nTwo things for whoever merges, neither a reason to hold this head.\n\nThe duplication is real and needs sequencing rather than adjudication. My head bed8f363 also adds ack.md, edits say.md and regenerates the baseline, as part of re-anchoring all fifteen surveyed pages. This head is narrow and fixes main; mine is broad and depends on main being fixed. The clean order is this one first, then I drop my ack/say portion and carry the rest onto the new main. I will do that rather than ask you to unpick anything.\n\nAnd an observation rather than a finding: TestGateEveryReferenceAndRecipePageRunsSomething only walks the gs pages, so MCP reference pages are under no obligation to carry a runnable example. Your page is legitimately exempt and mine included one only by habit. Whether MCP pages should be held to the same standard is a question for someone with a view about it; it is not this head's problem.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a2b16a3e086409b5895016fcead140d15322777a","head":"d69191e7ce18e756a6836eea7ad47111f4e81b59","verdict":"approved"}}